<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theognis of Megara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theognis of Megara was a Greek elegiac poet of the sixth century BCE. A collection of about 1,400 lines survives under his name, though much of it is by other poets. Addressed largely to a young man named Cyrnus, the poems give moral advice and voice the bitterness of an aristocrat facing social change and the rise of new wealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theognis-of-megara.jpg" alt="Theognis of Megara" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Émile Gilliéron · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red-figure_cup_with_symposiast_reciting_Theognis_(Athens,_NAM_1347),_drawn_by_%C3%89._Gilli%C3%A9ron_(AthMitt_1884,_pl_1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
